      "width": 1920,
      "height": 1536,
      "desc": "In Mongolian, “Tsagaan Idee” means “white foods,” a broad term for dairy products. Fresh milk is fermented, separated, and simmered into yogurt, milk curds, and butter—a craft still practiced in many herding households. In the families I visited, women handled the whole process, from milking to processing, relying on close knowledge of animals and experience with weather, temperature, and timing.\n            My camera lingered on grasslands, cattle, milk vessels, people indoors, and rows of dried curds; Moths, puddles, and scraps also entered the frame as part of daily life. I wanted to know, what kind of understanding of animals, land, and time is required to make the foods; and how this experience remains in life today."
